FINDINGS (none fails a written criterion; the lead adjudicates)
  F1 [SPEC-LEVEL, high user impact; follows from D2's "including its clamping"]
     A manual forward-then-back start-date change is now irreversible. On master a round trip gives back the same plan.
     Repro P4 (fixed plan, UpdateSettingsWithPersons 2026-09 -> 2027-09 -> 2026-09):
       want inc run0=0..24 soon=3 late=30, exp ends=0..6, ot=5
       got  inc run0=12..24 soon=12 late=30, exp ends=12..12, ot=5
     Result: 12 months of running income are lost, a future income is delayed 9 months, and a finished expense comes back at the far date.
     On master (base settings.go) P4 is an identity.
     The date input is hx-trigger="change delay:500ms", so every committed picker value is saved. Trying out a later start and coming back is enough to trigger this.
     The rollover only ever moves forward, so its clamping never met this case. Each single step still meets criterion 1, so this is the brief's rule and not a worker deviation.
  F2 [low] If the browser's month is ahead of the server's (the tick handler uses the browser's local `new Date()`), the tick or a direct POST shifts forward with clamping, then resolveCurrentMonth shifts back.
     Items at start 0 become 1 (P3: run0 0..24 -> 1..24, exp ends 0..6 -> 1..6). Master drifts EVERY item +1 on the same input, so the branch is still better.
     This cannot happen through the readonly server-rendered input of a use-current-month plan.
  F3 [pre-existing on master; backlog] A failed save leaks into the manager cache. When decodeSettings reports changed=true, loadInternal's saveInternal publishes the raw object as sm.cache (P9: "decode changed=true … loadInternal==cache: true"), and UpdateSettingsWithPersons then mutates it.
     P7: after a rejected save (birth month after start "1950-01"), Load() returns start=1950-01, while the disk still holds 2026-09.
     On master the leaked object has unshifted offsets; on the branch they are shifted to match. This breaks Load's documented "published object is immutable" contract.
  F4 [pre-existing; backlog] An unparseable start_date on the Rate Assumptions form comes back as HTTP 500, not 4xx. Branch and master are the same.
  Promotion candidates (V3 pattern): P1 and P2 (a use-current-month plan through UpdateSettingsWithPersons: stale disk and stale page) are not covered by any permanent test.

